description:
FILED by the s532 Fable merge review (2026-11-02), probes ~/pcl-agent-scratch/s532/review/merge-review/probes/a4081-3.pl (the divergence) and a4081-4.pl (the other order, = perl).  NOT a regression: base d47caabe crashed on the same program (%test-module-imported undefined), main b31345ca runs it with the wrong winner.  s530b's #4081 (%p-own-code-symbol: an install never redefines an INHERITED :pcl symbol; the Template::Test `ok` loop) is right; this is its residue.

    BEGIN { $INC{"My/T3.pm"} = 1; package My::T3; use Exporter "import"; our @EXPORT = qw(ok); sub ok { print "myok($_[1])\n"; 1 } }
    use My::T3; no warnings; use Test::More tests => 1;
    ok(1, "a");
    # perl: "ok 1 - a" (Test::More's ok wins: Exporter assigns the glob again, with a Prototype mismatch warning)
    # main: "myok(a)" then "# No tests run!" exit 255

MECHANISM: My::T3's import shadows `ok` in main (%p-own-code-symbol -> %p-symref-intern) and sets its fdefinition; Test::More is runtime-native (its `ok` is pcl::pl-ok, inherited), so its import installs nothing and the shadowing symbol keeps My::T3::ok.  The reverse order (the user import after Test::More) is right.  FIX SHAPE: the runtime-native import of NAME into PKG, when PKG already has an OWN (shadowing) symbol for NAME, re-points that symbol at the runtime function (the one place is %p-do-import's native-module arm, the inverse of %p-own-code-symbol) -- or unintern the shadow so the inherited symbol is visible again; compiled call sites read the symbol at load time, so probe a call compiled BEFORE the second import too.  LOW: a name conflict perl itself warns about; no board row behind it.


=== DONE (s532b) ===
FIXED (cl/pcl-test.lisp): %test-module-imported calls %test-more-rewin-exports -- for each name Test::More exports (*test-more-export-names*, the list %test-more-stash-install already used; Test::Simple: ok), an OWN (shadowing) symbol in the importing package is pointed at the TAP function: Exporter's glob assignment.  Probes = perl STDOUT: a4081-3's shape, the reverse order (user import after Test::More keeps the user's), a call compiled BEFORE the second import, a user `sub ok` after `use Test::More` (user's wins), an import into another package.  The #4081 shape stays right: Template-Toolkit t/evalperl.t one-file 23/0; Pl/t/method-name-clash-01.t green.  NOTE: the detector announces the transient install of the user module's `ok` on stderr (perl warns "Prototype mismatch" there too).  Guard Pl/t/method-name-clash-01.t last row.
